# Day 19 — Failsafe against lockups

## Report

On another computer (an image workstation) MView froze on a corrupt JPEG so badly that the process could not be ended.

A process that even Task Manager can't end usually has a thread stuck inside Windows (a disk / network read, a driver), or the computer is paging itself to death because one program asked for far more memory than it has. The second fits a corrupt JPEG: its header can claim up to 65535 x 65535 pixels, 17 GB as BGRA. (The file itself would help to find out exactly; please copy it to test\images\corrupt if possible.)

## Changes

**Emergency exit: Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Q** (new unit `source\utility\uWatchdog.pas`). A small thread of its own registers the key system-wide and ends the process at once (TerminateProcess) when it is pressed, whatever the window and the worker threads are doing. Shown in the context menu next to Exit.

**Exit deadline.** Every way of closing (Esc, gesture, menu, window button, settings editor) arms a 4 s deadline in the watchdog. A shutdown that waits longer, e.g. for a worker stuck in a decode, ends with the process being terminated instead of a process without a window left running. Settings are now saved before the threads are stopped, so they survive that.

**Size guard** (new unit `source\utility\uMemoryGuard.pas`). Before a bitmap is allocated, the loader checks that it fits: at most half of the physical memory for one image, never more than 2 gigapixels. Otherwise the image gets an error placeholder ("image too large for this computer: W x H would need N MB (limit M MB)") instead of the allocation. Applied to: JPEG (from the header, for the size actually decoded: 1/2..1/8 for the quick view, full size plus screen copy), EXIF thumbnails (more than 4 MP is not a thumbnail), TIFF through WIC (after WIC reports the size). Not yet: other formats read by BGRABitmap (PNG, BMP, GIF, TIFF fallback), which report their size only while decoding.

## What the failsafe can't do

If a thread is stuck in a driver or a read that Windows itself can't interrupt, no program can end the process until that read returns; neither the hotkey nor Task Manager. The size guard removes the most likely cause of that with corrupt files.

## To check

- Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Q while MView runs: it disappears at once.
- If another program already uses that key, the hotkey isn't available (TWatchdog.HotkeyRegistered is False); Esc and the deadline still work.
- A corrupt JPEG: error placeholder, browsing continues.

## The disk as the cause (user: "the most critical is likely the read from a corrupt or blocked disk")

A thread waiting in a read that Windows can't interrupt (bad sector being retried, hung USB or network drive) keeps the whole process alive until the read returns; no program can end it, Task Manager included. What MView can do: never let the window wait for the disk, notice a read that doesn't come back, go on without it. Three steps, all done:

**1. The window thread never reads folders** (spec §10, a shortcut taken in Phase B is gone):
- `TDirectoryTree.Build(..., ACollectImages)` lists every folder's images while it reads the folder anyway (the scanner already visited every folder).
- `TDirectoryScanner` does the whole opening: resolves the path (file or folder, root, start file), lists the start folder and delivers that first (`TScanStart`, `OnStartReady`), then the tree with all lists (`OnTreeReady`).
- `TNavigator.ReadsDisk := False` in the viewer: lists only from the scanner (`OpenListed`, the tree); a folder not known is simply not entered. The tests keep the old mode (ReadsDisk = True).
- `TMView.OpenMedia` only asks the scanner; "Opening ..." until it answers, "Not found" from the scanner. No FileExists / DirectoryExists on the window thread any more (drag and drop, start, last session).

**2. No file access per step:** the cache is asked by file name (`GetByName`) instead of by a key read from the disk (`MakeImageKey` did a FindFirst on every step). Opening and F5 clear the cache, since new lists may name other versions of the files. `TNavigator.ListedKey` gives size and date from the lists where a key is needed.

**3. Reads that don't come back:**
- Decode workers: every decoder calls its cancel check regularly (per band / 16-32 rows / file part); that call now also records "alive" (`TDecodeJob.WorkerCancelCheck`). A job not alive for 20 s is taken as stuck (`TJobScheduler.CheckStuck`, checked twice a second): the file gets the placeholder "Disk not responding (no answer for 20 s)", the job is cancelled, Windows is asked to cancel the read (`CancelSynchronousIo`, where the driver allows), the I/O gate is opened, and a replacement worker starts (at most 4 per session). If the stuck worker comes back later, it leaves if it was replaced, or simply goes on working; its image replaces the placeholder.
- Scanner: when it hasn't come back for 20 s the info line says "reading folders: the disk is not responding"; opening something else then starts a new scanner (at most 4).
- Exit: stuck threads are not waited for and what they may still use is not freed; the watchdog deadline ends the process (which Windows completes once the read returns; the window is gone at once).

Independent review found (and fixed): a worker pool that could shrink to nothing after repeated stalls; an unsigned-tick underflow that could mark a healthy worker stuck; a tree that could be delivered before its start and lost; shutdown double-free and use-after-free cases around stuck threads; the testing delay (DecodeDelayMs) counting as stuck; a leaked exception object (now EImageTooLarge); hotkey registration on systems without MOD_NOREPEAT.

