## Empty and damaged files (user question: "how does mview deal with 0 byte images?")

Before: safe but roundabout. A 0-byte .jpg is listed (by extension), has no preview, isn't recognised as JPEG and goes to BGRABitmap, which fails: error placeholder with BGRABitmap's message, browsing goes on (or the file is skipped with PlaceholderForBadImages=0). No hang, no allocation.

Now:
- **Empty file:** recognised at once in TMediaLoader.Load (size 0 from the key): "Empty file (0 bytes)", for every quality level, so it isn't asked for again.
- **Implausible JPEG size:** a header that claims more than 1000 pixels per byte of file ("Damaged file: the header claims W x H pixels, but the file has only N KB"). Even a black JPEG needs about a byte per 160 pixels, and a real 108 MP photo is about 10 MB against the 108 KB this would require. This closes a gap left by the memory guard: a claimed 65535 x 65535 fails the guard at full size (17 GB), but its 1/8 quick view (268 MB) would still have been decoded, as grey.

Test files in `test\images\broken` (see README.txt there): empty.jpg / .tif / .png, garbage.jpg (random bytes), header_only.jpg, truncated.jpg, jpeg_named.png (a JPEG with a .png name), huge_claim.jpg (main frame header patched to 65535 x 65535; its EXIF thumbnail is intact). Browsing through that folder must show placeholders (and a thumbnail for huge_claim.jpg, then the error) and never stall.

## Wheel press toggles fullscreen

Pressing the mouse wheel (middle button) switches fullscreen on / off, like Enter (uInputHandler, on the press; movement plays no part).

## LZW TIFF: own decoder

WIC needed 4.4-4.7 s for the quick view of a 108 MP LZW TIFF (9.2 s with two at once); Hamana a little under 3 s. The bands were already whole strips (1024 rows), so WIC wasn't re-decoding strips: it is simply slow for these files.

New in uTiffQuick: `LzwDecodeStrip` and `LoadLzwTiff` for LZW strip TIFFs (8-bit, chunky, RGB / RGBA / grey, predictor 1 or 2), quick view and full size:
- LZW decoding by position: each table entry is (position, length) of a string already in the output, so decoding is short copies inside the output buffer. One strip at a time (37 MB for these files; strips over 256 MB are left to WIC).
- Predictor 2 is undone per row, and only for rows that are used; the quick view uses 2 of every S rows (like the uncompressed reader) and averages all columns.
- Full size goes straight into the bitmap; memory guard as before.
- A damaged strip leaves the rest black; unreadable strip data or other layouts go to WIC.

Verified before porting: a Python copy of the exact algorithm decodes all 9 strips of IMG_20241126_201018.tif bit-identical to Pillow (predictor included). The Pascal port was checked against it by an independent review (one compile error found and fixed: a nested routine used an outer variable as loop counter).

Loader order for TIFF: uncompressed quick view (row skipping) -> LZW reader -> WIC -> FPReadTiff.

To check: the compressed folder, D line / timing.csv decode ms for `screen` and `full` (was 4400-4700 ms for the quick view).

## Window place remembered

`[Window] Left / Top / Width / Height` in MView.ini (the fields were in TConfig but never read or written). The main window reopens where it was, for the settings editor and the viewer's normal (not fullscreen) window, if that place is still on a monitor (one may have been unplugged). Fullscreen keeps it as the place to return to.
Saved (only these four keys, TConfig.SaveWindowBounds, so nothing else in the file changes):
- viewer: one second after the last move / resize, and on close;
- settings editor: on close and when "View images" starts the viewer. Not on every resize: the editor holds MView.ini as text, and its next Save would write the old values back (or a write to the file would clash with unsaved edits).
Not while maximised or minimised. Balloon help for the four keys.

## Skim came too rarely (TIFF folder)

The rule was a run of uninterrupted fast steps (each within 1/SkimRate s of the one before) lasting SkimEnterMs. Spinning a wheel has short pauses (moving the finger), and while large quick views are put on screen the window takes wheel events in bursts; either reset the run. Now: Skim while the steps of the last SkimEnterMs average SkimRate per second or more (a ring of the last 64 step times). Defaults unchanged (4 per second, 1500 ms: 6 steps within 1.5 s); it still ends after SkimExitMs (250 ms) without a step, and the next fast step takes it up again at once.

## Balloon help: value lists

SortMode and WrapScope list their possible values one per line, with their meaning and the default (the values can't be guessed). The editor's help bar is taller for that.

## LZW measured, then faster

First build of the own decoder (timing.csv 01:30): LZW quick view 3.76-3.87 s for the displayed image (the 7.8 s rows are preloads: their time includes waiting at the gate while the displayed image decoded). Uncompressed: 320-360 ms. The user: "compared to hamana the loading is really fast, factor 2-3 at least".
The same algorithm in C on this file: 1.5 s unoptimised, 0.75 s at -O2. So:
- `{$OPTIMIZATION ON}` (-O2) in uTiffQuick, whatever the project's level.
- Display jobs decode the strips in parallel: up to 3 helper threads (TLzwStripThread; cores - 1), each taking every n-th strip with its own file handle and buffers. Each writes only its own rows: full size straight into the bitmap (row pointers taken beforehand on one thread); quick view the used rows (2 per output row) into a buffer, which the calling worker then averages. Preloads stay single-threaded and keep giving way at the I/O gate. Cancel: the worker checks between its strips and sets an abort flag the helpers watch.
Expected: about 1 s for the LZW quick view of a 108 MP TIFF on 4 cores. To check: timing.csv decode_ms of the `compressed` folder.

## GIF animation

User: "please look at gif implementation now" (optimisation of the TIFF loaders left for phase G).

Design (spec §5.1, §8.7):
- **Own GIF decoder** (new unit `source\imaging\uGifDecoder.pas`) instead of BGRABitmap's reader: cancellable, memory-guarded, and a damaged or cut-off file shows / plays what it contains. Frames are kept as 8-bit palette indices plus a palette per frame, a quarter of BGRA (Test.gif: 126 frames of 800 x 704 = 70 MB instead of 284 MB). LZW by position (as for LZW TIFF), `{$OPTIMIZATION ON}`.
- **Quality levels:** Screen = the first frame only, read from the start of the file (8 MB, the whole file only if the first frame needs more). It is qlScreen if more frames follow, qlFull for a still GIF. Full = every frame (`TAnimation` on the IDecodedImage, Bitmap = the first frame), asked for after the usual RefineDelayMs pause (250 ms). So browsing and preloads never decode whole animations. Files named .gif that aren't GIFs go to BGRABitmap.
- **Playback** (new unit `source\imaging\uAnimation.pas`, TMView): a UI-thread timer; `TFrameClock` says which frame is due (fixed times from the start, so a late frame doesn't slow the animation; skipped frames are counted; after a stall of more than 1 s it goes on from now); the animation's cursor builds the frame (a GIF frame usually changes only a rectangle; disposal 1/2/3, transparency) and it goes to the renderer as a new version of the same image, so zoom, pan and rotation stay. While the GPU is still uploading the previous frame (`TRenderer.IsUploading`), no frame is built: the clock skips instead of queueing. The clock knows nothing about GIF (frame delays through a function), so movies without sound can use it later (rule M6).
- Loops forever (loop counts in the file are ignored); no playback while skimming (starts when Skim ends); stops when another file is shown. Delay 0 or 1 plays at 100 ms (as browsers do). Transparent pixels show black.
- Info line: "animated, N frames". D line: "frame i / N, k skipped", and a note if the memory limit (a quarter of RAM, at most 2 GB) left frames out.

Verified before porting: a Python copy of the algorithm (`test\images\gif\gifproto.py`) is identical to Pillow frame by frame on Test.gif, three other real GIFs and synthetic ones (disposal 2 and 3, transparency, interlaced, local palettes, frames outside the logical screen, ffmpeg output, cut off). The Pascal port was compared against it line by line by an independent review, which also re-ran it (ported to Python) on all test files: identical; cut at every length and 400 random corruptions: no out-of-bounds access. It found no compile errors; fixed from it: a memory-limit test that could not fail as written, an animation that kept playing (and holding its frames) after stepping to a file not yet decoded, a zero-length copy on a cut-off sub-block, and the memory guard now counts everything held while the first frame is made.

