## GIF test suite and NASA images (user: "please check the test folder")

TestGif: 156 passed (user's run 02:14). The test folder now also has a GIF test suite (test\images\gif: animated_*.gif, static_*.gif, reference frames in gif\frames) and three NASA Blue Marble images of 21600 x 21600 (467 megapixels) in test\images\super images.

GIF test suite: every frame of every file matches its reference picture, after two changes:
- **The picture is the logical screen size;** frames are clipped to it (animated_transparent_loop_frames_out_of_bounds expects 100 x 100, not enlarged to the frame at x = 200 as Pillow does). Only a logical screen of 0 x 0 takes the first frame's size. frame_outside_canvas.gif is now 200 x 150 (expected.txt updated).
- **Loop counts are honoured** (NETSCAPE2.0 / ANIMEXTS1.0): 0 = forever, n = n repeats after the first play; without the extension (animated_noloop) the animation plays once and stays on its last frame. TFrameClock takes a play count and reports Finished; the D line says "done". Showing the file again plays it again.
- A frame claiming more than the memory guard allows, or more than 4 times the picture (at least 16 MP), is damage: the file ends there (the first frame: error).
TestGif compares every suite frame with its PNG (transparent pixels by transparency only) and checks the loop counts; clock tests for 1 and 2 plays.

NASA images (timing.csv 02:14-02:16):
- **JPG:** quick view 3.3 s (own decoder at 1/8), fine.
- **PNGs: error after about 21 s.** PNG went to BGRABitmap: always the full 467 MP (1.9 GB) with no size check first and no cancel; it reports no progress while decoding, so most likely the stuck-read watchdog took it for a hung disk after 20 s (the error text wasn't recorded). Now PNG and BMP are read like TIFF: **WIC in bands** (about 8 MB per call), cancellable and "alive" between bands, the quick view averaged down while reading (only a screen-size bitmap), Full after the memory guard. BGRABitmap remains the fallback when WIC can't read a file, now with the **memory guard before loading** (size from the PNG / BMP / GIF header), which closes the gap noted at the start of today.

